  <h2 id="section-2">2. What is Base64 and How Does It Work?</h2>
  <p>
    At its core, <strong>Base64</strong> is a group of binary-to-text encoding algorithms designed to represent binary data in an ASCII string format. It is universally used across computer systems to transmit raw multimedia files through text-based protocols such as HTTP, SMTP (email), and JSON APIs without risk of data corruption.
  </p>

  <h3>How the 64-Character Encoding Works</h3>
  <p>
    Computers process binary data in bytes consisting of 8 bits each (values from 0 to 255). Many binary byte sequences represent non-printable control characters that can break text parsers. Base64 solves this by:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Taking <strong>3 binary bytes (24 bits)</strong> of source data at a time.</li>
    <li>Splitting those 24 bits into <strong>4 groups of 6 bits</strong> each (where each 6-bit chunk has a value from 0 to 63).</li>
    <li>Mapping each 6-bit value to a safe 64-character alphabet: uppercase letters (<code>A–Z</code>), lowercase letters (<code>a–z</code>), digits (<code>0–9</code>), plus (<code>+</code>), and slash (<code>/</code>), using equals (<code>=</code>) for end padding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Standard Image URLs vs. Base64 Data URIs</h3>
  <p>
    To understand the architectural difference, compare how a web browser loads both approaches:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Standard URL Reference:</strong> <code>&lt;img src="https://example.com/assets/logo.png"&gt;</code><br />
      <em>Workflow:</em> Browser requests HTML &rarr; finds URL &rarr; triggers separate DNS lookup and HTTP round-trip &rarr; downloads image &rarr; renders graphic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Base64 Data URI:</strong> <code>&lt;img src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUA..."&gt;</code><br />
      <em>Workflow:</em> Browser requests HTML &rarr; decodes embedded text string in memory &rarr; renders graphic instantaneously without an additional network request.
    </li>
  </ul>

